<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>NFD</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the impact of different timesteps on optimization, we keep other hyperparameters constant and test 10, 20, and 40 steps. When the number of denoising steps is increased to 40, performance improved only slowly and marginally; the best results were observed with 10–20 steps.
Regarding noise level, we test 0.25, 0.5 and 0.75. Setting a too low noise level diminishes performance gains, while results remain similar for noise levels between 0.5 and 0.75.
Table <a href="#A2.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ B.2 NFD ‣ Appendix B Ablation Studies ‣ Reinforcement Learning with Inverse Rewards for World Model Post-training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and <a href="#A2.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ B.2 NFD ‣ Appendix B Ablation Studies ‣ Reinforcement Learning with Inverse Rewards for World Model Post-training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> shows the effect of denoising steps and noise level.</p>
</div>
